This is very similar to OAK-955. 
The query engine generates a filter that doesn&apos;t have all the fulltext conditions present, that is why the lucene index returns 1070 results.

fyi the query is
{code}
SELECT * FROM [nt:base] as NODE WHERE LOWER(localname(NODE)) like &apos;sql2nodelocalnametest%&apos; OR contains(NODE.*, &apos;sql2nodelocalnametest&apos;)
{code}

A workaround for queries that have two property conditions is to use &quot;double negation&quot; as in:

{noformat}
(a) where property1 = &apos;x&apos; and property2 = &apos;y&apos;
(b) where property1 = &apos;x&apos; and not not property2 = &apos;y&apos;
{noformat}

Those two queries are equivalent (return the same nodes), but query (a) could use an index on property2 (if there is one), while query (b) can not, because the query engine doesn&apos;t currently optimize the &quot;not not&quot; condition.

